00:01:50.336 --> 00:01:52.100
to Kubernetes and GKE.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.150
But serverless really
enables us to concentrate

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.850
on the migration and not the
standing up of the DevOps.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.530
We just want to be able
to migrate and scale.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.190
So let's kick off and start
looking at our monolith.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.300
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: All right.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.090
So monolith applications have
several things in common.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.900
They tend to be
standalone, isolated.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.400
They're self-contained
in the sense

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.920
that all the logic's contained
within a single instance

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.330
or a single unit.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:23.100
And they tend to be large and
very complex to work with.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.220
And a lot of the time,
it's even difficult

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.780
to figure out where
do you actually

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.160
start when you
want to understand

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.110
how your application works.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.590
So we realize that this is
a very opinionated topic,

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.600
and there's a lot of
content out there.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.660
We're going to be focusing on
showing you a few examples,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.550
showing you a few
techniques on how

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:45.180
to break off different
parts of your monolith,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.010
convert them to microservices.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.500
And hopefully this is
something you can take

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.820
with you to your organization.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.037
So we're going to start with--

00:02:54.037 --> 00:02:55.620
as an example, we're
going to be using

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.690
an e-commerce application.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.290
Why an e-commerce application?

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.660
Well, we think that a lot of the
properties in this application

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.660
resemble other
monoliths out there.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.570
So there's a user interface
that's generated for the user

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.030
to see and interact with.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.300
We have a load balancer
that handles requests

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.030
from different locations
and distributes them

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.950
among instances.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.730
All the application logic is
contained within a single unit.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.300
State is associated within
the instance itself.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.790
And in terms of
storage, well, you're

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.070
probably using
something really ancient

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.040
as a catch-all solution
for all your data,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.110
maybe something like
a relational database.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.920
So let's take a
step back and think.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.650
When do you actually want
to move to microservices?

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.030
What motivates it?

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.120
There are several good
candidate applications that

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.340
are good for microservices.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.970
Web applications, for example.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:00.080
They tend to be simple
but require the ability

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.800
to handle a lot of traffic,
so they need to scale.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.310
Enterprise applications tend
to be a lot more complex, very

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.680
huge, and they tend
to be applications

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.180
that need to do everything.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.290
But what drives it?

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.147
In the end, it's the
business requirements.

00:04:14.147 --> 00:04:15.980
Essentially, what does
the business actually

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.510
need to succeed?

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.149
Does it need the application
to be scalable such

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:24.440
that it can handle increased
requests and volumes of data?

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.350
Or do you need to be able to
optimize the application such

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.840
that you would only
be using the resources

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.280
that you actually need?

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.750
Or is it more about being able
to remain competitive and have

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.530
the ability to develop
features very quickly, so you

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:41.930
need high development velocity?

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.410
There are a lot of reasons.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.520
Let's look at an example
to showcase this better.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.100
This is our monolith.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.870
And it has all the
main components,

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:56.310
has a UI, a load balancer,
application, an application

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.730
instance, and a database.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:03.360
And imagine that you have a
celebrity that tweets about one

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.070
of the products
that you're selling

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.040
on your e-commerce application.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.910
And let's say that
tweet goes viral.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.590
So suddenly, you
have a huge number

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.670
of requests coming in for people
searching for that product,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.190
wanting to put it
in a shopping cart,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.540
and maybe purchasing it as well.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.890
So you need to be able to
handle this increase in volume

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.000
of requests.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:24.810
What do you do?

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.980
You scale.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.720
You can scale vertically.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.820
That will only take
you so far, so you

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.417
have to scale horizontally.

00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:33.500
This is how it would look.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.650
Basically, you're
adding instances.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.580
But think about this for a sec.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.800
What are you doing here?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.975
You're replicating the whole
application on each instance.

00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:46.590
Do you actually need that?

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.150
The scenario might only need
you to scale certain bits,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.370
but you're scaling the
whole application, which

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.930
means you're using
a lot of resources

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.750
that you don't actually need.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.360
And at the same time, we
said that state is associated

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.100
with the application instance.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.000
How do you do that?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.640
Well, you can set
up the load balancer

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.400
to have a sticky session
with that instance

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.250
for every specific user.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.870
What if that application
instance falls over?

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.390
Then everything's
lost for that user.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:15.850
You could move the
state to the database,

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.650
but then you're overwhelming
the database even more.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:22.510
And if that falls over, you have
a whole set of other problems

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.400
to deal with.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.110
So imagine that you also want
to be able to add features.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.450
And let's say you
do a small UI tweak,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.150
but because this is a
monolith, you actually

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.490
have to deploy the
whole application.

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.980
And let's say you
made a mistake.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.670
Your whole application goes down
because of a simple UI tweak,

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:45.710
and there's nothing
you can do about it.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:49.650
The thing is here the main
idea behind microservices

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.180
is that it helps
you bypass all that,

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.640
because it gives you ability
to do partial deployments.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.052
It's published in
small iterations.

00:06:59.052 --> 00:07:00.760
And it gives the
ability to better handle

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.110
scale and optimization.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.180
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
So all good reasons

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.670
to move to microservices,
but should we

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.380
microservice the world?

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.980
I mean, does everything
need to be in microservices?

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.476
So this is a talk
about migration

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:17.100
to microservices,
but one of the things

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.620
you've got to do, you've got to
make sure that you sense check,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.411
that you're not just
following the internet

00:07:21.411 --> 00:07:24.510
or what somebody else has done.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.370
If you're not trying to
fix a problem of scale

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.510
or optimization, then
what are your motivations

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.180
to go into microservices?

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.450
Is it a complexity thing?

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:35.997
Well, microservices
may not actually

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:37.080
make your life any easier.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.870
I'm sure most of you
are aware of that.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.810
You might be running specialist
software or hardware or maybe

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.190
both.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.320
Would you then go
to microservices?

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.080
What are you actually gaining?

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:53.110
And just like you've got to have
the business reasons to move

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.770
to microservices, well, let's
think of the business reasons

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.660
to stick with a monolith.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.210
If your monolith is serving
the business well, it's secure,

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:07.500
it's stable, then what is
the business motivation?

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.150
And I think anybody
who undertakes

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.910
this journey is going to
require a lot of money, time,

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.310
and emotional toil.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.590
You must make sure that
you sense check first.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:21.630
But with our example, with our
web app, our e-commerce app,

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.505
we're taking a scenario
that we actually-- it

00:08:23.505 --> 00:08:25.020
was built some time ago.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.760
And we had a regional
customer base.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.940
And now we've gone
global, which is great.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:32.730
But the problem is
our system's now

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.700
suffering from stability
and deployment issues

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.980
that Hussain has
just highlighted.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:42.600
We've also noticed that
our customer competition

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:43.860
is outperforming us.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.140
They're deploying new
features faster than us,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.330
and they're eating
our breakfast.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.760
So we have plenty
of justification

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:52.240
to move to microservices.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:53.100
So let's begin.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.800
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: Yeah.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.910
So what we're going
to do is start

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.040
by focusing on capabilities.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.480
What are capabilities?

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.360
They're essentially what
the application does

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.420
or what the code does,
not what the code is.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.305
Code can be
rewritten, refactored.

00:09:10.305 --> 00:09:11.930
A lot of the time,
you're going to have

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.790
to rewrite stuff from scratch.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.150
That's not the point here.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.330
What we need to
focus on is being

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:19.220
able to take these
capabilities that

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.860
are in your current
monolithic application

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.960
and move them to your
microservices application.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:28.080
So that's going to be the
focus of this discussion.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.145
So what capabilities are
we going to talk about?

00:09:31.145 --> 00:09:33.050
We're going to talk
about a bunch that are

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.630
common among monoliths as well?

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:38.600
Storage, do we actually
stick with one database

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:39.536
or look at several?

00:09:39.536 --> 00:09:40.910
We're going to be
discussing what

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.110
we call as edge capabilities.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.640
These are ones that can easily
be detached from the monolith.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.040
We're also going to be talking
about sticky capabilities.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.230
These keep you stuck
to the monolith.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:55.610
And we're going to also talk
about networking in the sense

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.370
that how the different
parts of the application

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.670
actually talk to each other.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:06.310
So let's take a closer look
at data and our data store.

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:09.400
In our monolithic application,
in the e-commerce application,

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.820
we had one data
store for everything,

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.740
which is relational.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.750
What happens when you
have both an increase

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.950
in the number of requests
and the amount of data?

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.690
You need to be able
to scale to handle,

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:25.060
but it's relational, so you
can only probably scale it

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:26.200
vertically.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:27.910
I've actually seen
customers trying

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:33.520
to mimic horizontal scaling
by creating multiple database

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.780
instances and then sharding
the data across them

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.780
and then writing some
logic to try to figure out

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.220
where the request would go.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:40.250
That's not a good idea.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:41.630
Don't do that.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.660
And I've also seen
some customers

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:46.540
that actually take
a NoSQL database,

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:49.300
but then use that as a
catch all solution, which

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.480
still doesn't help, and
try to write some SQL

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.530
queries over that.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:57.120
That also doesn't
work very well.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.850
The idea is you
need to think about

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.590
the problem you're
trying to solve

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.800
and the right solution for it.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.240
It could be multiple solutions,
not a single solution.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.220
So what do we mean by that?

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:14.936
Imagine that you have a huge
increase in the number of users

00:11:14.936 --> 00:11:16.060
and the number of products.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.730
Let's say a few million
users and hundreds

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.620
of millions of products.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:22.364
How do you deal with that?

00:11:22.364 --> 00:11:23.030
What do you use?

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.400
How do you store that?

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.560
You can use a NoSQL
database, something

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.510
like a document database.

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.790
That would fit very well,
because for each you

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.950
can have these little
documents, and each document

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:37.780
would be the user or the product
and any number of properties.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.280
That's a good fit.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.270
So that's one solution.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:43.180
What if you want to be able to--

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:47.390
let's say, be able to
retrieve the 10,000 or 1,000

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.630
most popular products?

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:51.130
Then you need an
in-memory database,

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:55.501
something that acts like a
cache for your hard data.

00:11:55.501 --> 00:11:58.000
What if you want to be able to
see what the user is actually

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.580
doing in terms of where
are they clicking,

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:04.330
what are they searching for,
what are they purchasing,

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.540
and maybe use that data to
try to predict what they're

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.390
going to be doing, predict what
they're going to be buying?

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.060
That's where a data warehouse
solution would fit, something

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.530
like BigQuery.

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:17.650
What about the other
stuff that might still

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.110
fit well in your SQL database?

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:24.690
Stuff like transactions,
which relational databases

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.980
are kind of fit for.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:30.970
You can keep using that for
those kinds of transactions.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.710
But look at a managed solution.

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:36.180
There are a lot of
those on the cloud now.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.370
One thing also to
mention is that do

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.760
we want to actually
start by implementing all

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.700
these different NoSQL solutions,
put in an in-memory database

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.300
right away, build up or
set up a data warehouse?

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.880
That seems like a lot of
work, and you don't really

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.380
want to do this in one go.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:56.010
You want to do it in
an iterative way such

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.380
that you're not
overwhelm yourself.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.750
So the idea is you
have all your data

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:02.500
sitting in your legacy store.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.120
And as you move forward, you
chip away different parts,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:09.070
find the right solution
to solve that problem,

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:10.860
and use that solution
solve that problem.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:15.860
What about edge capabilities?

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:20.480
These are capabilities
that are easy to migrate.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.060
These are sort your the
low-hanging fruit or easy wins,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.620
let's say.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.750
They have certain
properties that kind of

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.220
make them stand up.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:30.410
One thing is that they're
standalone in the sense

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:33.410
that if you detach
them from the monolith

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.570
or break them off the
monolith, they don't really

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:37.490
break the monolith itself.

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:40.040
At the same time, they have
minimal dependency back

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.542
to the monolith.

00:13:41.542 --> 00:13:42.875
So let's look at a few examples.

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:48.600
Image upload, that seems
like an easy thing to do.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:51.075
All you do is
basically take an image

00:13:51.075 --> 00:13:53.610
and push it to a
storage location.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.385
That can be a service on its own
that just responds to requests.

00:13:56.385 --> 00:13:58.224
It can even be an
external service.

00:13:58.224 --> 00:13:59.640
That's something
that's not really

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.630
tied that much to the
e-commerce app that we have.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.990
Another example of
thumbnail creation.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:07.710
That's also something
that can easily

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.990
be detached without
much dependency

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.610
back to the monolith.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:15.300
Both of these are easy
services to do that.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.565
Let's look at something
a bit more complex.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:24.090
We're going to be looking
at one that is not easy.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:27.450
It tends to be everywhere when
it shouldn't be everywhere.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:28.000
Any ideas?

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:28.940
Is

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:33.100
It's going to be your
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:34.590
What is that essentially?

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.250
That's your UI.

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:37.640
Think of it this way.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.865
You have this big
monolithic application

00:14:39.865 --> 00:14:42.520
and in different parts
of that application,

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.760
depending on the feature
or the capability,

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:51.100
you're going to have HTML,
CSS, JavaScript embedded there.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:54.370
And the UI that the
user interacts with

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:58.240
is kind of a combination of all
that HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.910
from all across your
monolithic application.

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:02.450
So what do you do here?

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:05.900
How do we actually break
this off to microservices?

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:09.380
So the idea is we need to
have all that HTML, CSS,

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:11.800
and JavaScript--
basically all your code--

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.600
in one service.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.660
So lets do that.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:20.680
Here we have it, a
monolith and a new service

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:24.610
called the front-end UI that's
running all of your UI logic.

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:28.990
It has all the CSS,
JavaScript, and HTML.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:30.920
But there's a problem here.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.560
Any ideas?

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:36.130
The thing is it doesn't actually
tie back to your monolith.

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:38.140
It's a standalone service.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:39.430
So it's static.

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:41.510
The UI doesn't
really do anything.

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:43.180
So how do we deal with this?

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:45.540
We need some sort of API
layer on the monolith

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.209
such that front-end UI can
interact with that there.

00:15:48.209 --> 00:15:50.500
And that layer would interact
with the different pieces

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.870
in the monolith, maybe
how it's showcased here

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:54.750
and the different capabilities.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:58.140
So how would something
like that look like?

00:15:58.140 --> 00:15:59.760
Something like
this, where you're

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.130
looking at each capability
or each part of the monolith

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:05.760
where you extracted that
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:10.550
and have some sort of API
interface to interact with it.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:11.690
That seems pretty neat.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:13.840
We didn't really have to
change that much code,

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:17.120
and we managed to separate
the service all on its own.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.480
But there's a problem here.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.100
Does anybody know what that is?

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:22.919
Maybe Justin knows what that is.

00:16:22.919 --> 00:16:24.460
JUSTIN GRAYSTON:
OK, so this takes us

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.860
onto sticky capabilities.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.200
And it sounds a bit
gross really, doesn't it?

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:32.366
But what do we mean by
sticky capabilities?

00:16:32.366 --> 00:16:34.240
Well, they're hard to
pull from the monolith.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:35.980
If you can imagine
a piece of gum,

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:36.940
and you're trying
to take it, you're

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:38.750
always being pulled
back to that monolith.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:39.250
,

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:40.940
Now why are we
being pulled back?

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.330
Well, the code might
take an approach

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.010
where if you had
nice modular code,

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:46.718
you can go and stick
that and create that

00:16:46.718 --> 00:16:49.455
into a sort of service,
maybe not a microservice,

00:16:49.455 --> 00:16:50.455
but that could be great.

00:16:50.455 --> 00:16:54.860
But actually, some capabilities
here, you know, monoliths?

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:59.260
That code is not necessarily
nicely encapsulated.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:02.440
So we've also got state.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.645
State could be in-memory
in the monolith.

00:17:04.645 --> 00:17:06.670
It could be in the
database, you know?

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.310
If we want to
create a service, we

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:13.319
need to be able to have
that stateless service,

00:17:13.319 --> 00:17:15.295
and we need to be
able to not have

00:17:15.295 --> 00:17:16.420
to go back to the monolith.

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:18.940
We want to actually move the
capability completely away

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:20.609
from that monolith.

00:17:20.609 --> 00:17:22.980
OK, so these were
the capabilities

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:24.300
we got in our system.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:27.210
We've got rid of
the UI, the main UI.

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:28.820
That's gone.

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:31.087
Hussain's also found a
couple of edge capabilities.

00:17:31.087 --> 00:17:32.670
There may be more
than that, but we're

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:34.490
going to get rid of those.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:36.470
Who's near a microphone?

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:39.000
Right, who wants to take a shot?

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.620
And this is really high level.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.000
There's no detail of the system.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.580
Who wants to maybe
point out what

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:48.090
they think might be a sticky
capability in the system?

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:49.470
Anybody?

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.636
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: Come on.

00:17:50.636 --> 00:17:51.287
Don't be shy.

00:17:51.287 --> 00:17:52.620
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Authentication.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.550
Yeah, great Yeah,
OK, maybe search.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.040
Well, authentication, that
was the one I was looking for.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:00.390
And we're going to come to that.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:04.410
The reason why is
because of the state.

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:06.360
So there's a few others
that we identified.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.810
Now, we'd hope that,
you know, if you've

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:12.110
got teams working on a monolith,
they should know the code.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:14.145
So there should
be some easy wins.

00:18:14.145 --> 00:18:15.520
They should really
understand it.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.110
But for this point, for
the point of this exercise,

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:19.610
some of these are
really high level.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.220
I mean, what does
schedule tasks do?

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:25.280
I mean, that could be Bash
scripts doing who knows what,

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.580
which is absolutely critical
for the monolith to stand up.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.820
How does that turn into a
microservice service, you know?

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:35.990
Stock tracking, shipment
management, returns management.

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:37.641
That all sounds very stateful.

00:18:37.641 --> 00:18:39.140
We're going to have
to track things.

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.440
Maybe we're having to
talk to other systems.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.966
All of these things are
not edge capabilities.

00:18:44.966 --> 00:18:46.340
All of these
things, you're going

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.530
to have to look at
breaking them down

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:51.410
and really understand
what it is they need to do

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:54.920
and how we can move the state
to somewhere else, because we

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:57.140
don't want to be coming
back to this monolith.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.860
The other one is
whatever the databases.

00:18:59.860 --> 00:19:02.810
And in our case, it's this
single large relational

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:05.800
database.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:10.860
I've seen a couple of
migrations from monoliths

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:16.360
to microservices, where all
the monolith code is completely

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:17.065
gone.

00:19:17.065 --> 00:19:20.050
And maybe there's
people here that will

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:22.000
know this kind of scenario.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:22.980
All that code's gone.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:24.460
Monolith is deprecated.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.680
But what's still there?

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:30.370
In the middle of that is
one single large database,

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:33.520
which is actually the phantom
legacy of the monolith,

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:37.630
because it wasn't tackled
while you migrated.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:40.570
And although we
don't want to do--

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:42.880
as Hussain pointed out,
we don't want to go ah,

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:44.742
there's all these
database technologies.

00:19:44.742 --> 00:19:45.700
It's going to be great.

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.490
Every single microservice,
let's use another one.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:49.730
We don't want to do that.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:51.370
But what we do
want to do is as we

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:54.580
start to pull these
hard services out,

00:19:54.580 --> 00:19:56.080
we need to really
consider how we

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:57.880
move that state
to somewhere else,

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:01.800
like whether it's
in-memory, NoSQL.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:04.750
But you want to consider
that as you go, right?

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:07.240
Don't try and do it all at once.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:11.270
And then we got
user auth points.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:12.200
So user auth.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.630
The monolith is handling log in.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.130
It's handling log out.

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:18.290
It's handling
session management.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:20.880
So how are we going
to deal with that?

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:22.990
Well, let's take that one first.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:27.860
So we had this notion that
we can put APIs everywhere.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:29.500
But there's a big
problem with this.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:31.708
Well, we haven't actually
moved it from the monolith.

00:20:31.708 --> 00:20:33.190
We haven't actually
done anything.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.380
We've just created an
API here, and we really

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.450
want to make sure that
our platform going forward

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.110
is on the left-hand side,
not in the monolith.

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:43.790
So let's move it over there.

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:44.600
That was very easy.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:48.140
I press the clicker and now
my user auth's over there.

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:50.330
I want one of these.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:51.900
So how do we do that?

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.410
Well, if you are lucky
and your monolith

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:58.160
has nice encapsulated
code, all nice and modular,

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.380
maybe you could
just lift and create

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:05.690
a mini monolith, which just
handles the authentication.

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:07.340
You could still
use that database.

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:10.640
Maybe you could take the user
tables out of that database

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.070
and just have a smaller
relational database.

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:17.240
You know, you just keep that
as a domain-specific database

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.900
We didn't do that.

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:22.330
We decided to use Firebase auth.

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:24.790
And this is not necessarily a
pitch just for Firebase auth

00:21:24.790 --> 00:21:26.680
just because it's Google.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:28.180
You know, you could
choose anything.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:30.790
But what we chose is
to move completely away

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:32.380
from the monolith.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.360
Now, we've got all
these users over here,

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.040
but with Firebase,
we can use the CLI

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:40.360
tool to import those existing
users into Firebase auth.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:43.900
Now, the reason why we chose
this is because, actually, it

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.940
was a lot less work, and
it gave us extra benefits.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:50.470
So it fully broke us
away from the monolith.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:53.440
We're no longer going back to
that monolith for anything.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:56.740
It gives us more login options
for very little effort.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:59.350
We didn't have native apps
before, but now we can.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:02.500
And we've got one single
authorization plane.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:05.200
And it potentially gives
us real-time possibilities

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.690
in the future and our
clients if we wanted to.

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:09.590
So how does this actually work?

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:10.690
Well, we have a user API.

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:13.360
We've created an
interface that everything

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:17.330
is going to use going forward
for your user authentication.

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:20.680
And on there, it's basically
using the Firebase Admin

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.780
SDK to authorize.

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:24.370
We've got another
little benefit here,

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:27.310
because our monolith was
using session cookies,

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.270
but Firebase uses JWT tokens.

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:31.540
And JWT tokens are
a really great way

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:35.100
to parse identity between
microservice calls.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:38.820
Now, session cookie
would not be so great.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.830
So that's what we chose to do.

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:43.320
But we have another problem.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.330
It's always problems, isn't it?

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:48.660
We have a problem now, because
we created all those APIs

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:51.120
for the quick wins for all
the different capabilities

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.260
in the monolith to get
our front-end UI working.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:54.990
And what we've done
is we've broken it

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:57.810
by taking user auth
out of the monolith.

00:22:57.810 --> 00:22:59.010
Not great.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:00.634
So how do we fix that?

00:23:00.634 --> 00:23:02.550
Well, we thought of a
few different scenarios.

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.070
We could put the Firebase Admin
SDK all over the monolith.

00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:08.400
That sounded like a pain.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:14.030
What we chose to do is we
altered the monolithic code

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:19.460
to be remote procedure calls
out to our new user service.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.500
And what this has done is now
that's flipped the situation.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.040
Instead of auth being
pulled back to the monolith,

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:31.060
now the monolith is going to
our new service environment.

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:33.300
That's a big win,
because once you've

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:38.740
done that, it makes it easier
to migrate other services.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:41.950
There's one more
thing to do with auth.

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:44.670
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: So one
important thing to remember

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:47.520
is that auth is atomic.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:48.616
What does that mean?

00:23:48.616 --> 00:23:51.630
Let's take a step back
and think about this.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:55.860
Every request coming
into your application

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.930
needs to be authenticated.

00:23:57.930 --> 00:24:01.620
And in fact, a lot of the
requests between services

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:03.730
need to be
authenticated as well.

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:04.230
Why?

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:07.650
Because now you don't have
one monolithic application.

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:09.300
You have these
microservices talking

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:11.310
to each other over a network.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:15.540
So there's more into it in
terms of the added network

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.820
latency because of this.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:21.490
Network latency
has a huge impact

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:23.990
on how your application
would behave.

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:26.090
Think of this scenario.

00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:30.110
You have your
monolithic application,

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:32.550
and whenever you
send a request to it,

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:37.160
it responded at a certain
rate or with a certain delay.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.680
Now that you went
to microservices,

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:43.190
you're actually
seeing a bigger delay.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:45.172
That shouldn't be
the case, but why?

00:24:45.172 --> 00:24:46.880
It's because of the
latency that's added.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:49.570
There are a number of things
we need to think about.

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:52.510
How many services do you
have talking to each other?

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:55.450
Do you have a continuous
chain of one service calling

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:56.320
the other?

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:59.125
That would introduce
a lot of latency.

00:24:59.125 --> 00:25:01.840
You need to think
about location.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:05.380
Are your services talking to
each other across the Atlantic

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.630
or are they within
a specific region?

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:10.480
They should be within a
region, because having

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:14.159
them spread apart would add
a lot of latency as well.

00:25:14.159 --> 00:25:15.700
You need to take
advantages of things

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:19.390
like caching, because
if you have services

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:22.180
that communicate a lot
with each other, each time,

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:23.440
that's latency.

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:25.630
That's added more latency.

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.380
So taking advantage of some
caching would reduce that.

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:31.420
And at the same time,
you need to make

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:34.570
sure you have really good
monitoring and alerting,

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:36.767
because things will go wrong.

00:25:36.767 --> 00:25:38.350
And when they go
wrong, you need to be

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:40.210
able to figure out
what went wrong,

00:25:40.210 --> 00:25:42.740
because you have a much more
complex system now with,

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:44.749
like we said,
services interacting

00:25:44.749 --> 00:25:46.040
with each other over a network.

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:47.837
So you need good
monitoring and alerting

00:25:47.837 --> 00:25:48.920
to be able to detect that.

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:55.860
We talked a lot about how
to actually take a monolith,

00:25:55.860 --> 00:26:01.160
break out some pieces off, and
turn them into microservices.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:03.620
In the beginning, Justin
said we should recommend

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:05.630
using a serverless
platform, something

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.610
that's completely managed.

00:26:07.610 --> 00:26:10.930
But we see that
there's an opportunity

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.480
to talk about
event-driven architecture,

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:15.340
because it gives
you the capability

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:18.250
to do things very quickly,
especially since we're

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:20.260
rewriting a lot of stuff.

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:21.880
Using event-driven
architecture can

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:25.930
help us add a lot of features or
a lot of services very quickly.

00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:27.505
Let's look at a few examples.

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:34.384
This is a case where
we are creating a user.

00:26:34.384 --> 00:26:35.050
So what happens?

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:37.420
You get a request,
create request.

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.490
That goes to the user API.

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:44.360
And the user API does its
thing and updates the database.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:46.130
At the same time, it
kicks off a message

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:48.350
through our messaging
queue, pops up.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:51.950
And that triggers
off a few functions.

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:55.300
There's one function that
sends out a welcome email

00:26:55.300 --> 00:26:57.040
through the mailer API.

00:26:57.040 --> 00:27:01.660
Another function signs the
user up for the newsletter.

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:05.210
And to be honest, you can
add more and more functions

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:06.360
very easily.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:08.270
And think about this here.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:12.170
Each function is actually
acting as its own microservice,

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:17.180
because it's isolated from
the rest of the application,

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.370
and it's providing a service.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.070
And it can scale based on
the demand, because you're

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:24.350
doing it on functions, which
is a serverless, scalable

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:26.270
platform.

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:29.650
Let's look at another example.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:31.120
You're uploading an image.

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:35.320
That image, let's say, is
uploaded to objects storage

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:37.120
or something like GCS.

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:38.710
And that results in a trigger.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:40.940
Also kicks off a
bunch of functions.

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:44.990
One function to store image
URLs in the user profile.

00:27:44.990 --> 00:27:47.920
Another function creates
thumbnails from that image.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:50.470
And again, you can add
more and more functions.

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:52.390
And each one of these
is acting as its

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:55.780
on microservice and scalable,
because you're running it

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:57.160
on a serverless platform.

00:27:57.160 --> 00:27:58.480
That was very easy to do.

00:28:01.634 --> 00:28:03.300
There's also an
opportunity here to talk

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:06.270
about event sourcing,
which is essentially

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:10.260
when you want to be able to
update state and update state

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:13.350
in different locations
within in a sequence.

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.750
So look at this as an example.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.010
User purchases something.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:20.410
That creates a payment
notification, which

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:22.150
tells the card service,
hey, this product

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:25.450
has been purchased, or this
item has been purchased.

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:27.940
That marks it and
basically kicks off

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.970
a message that triggers
a few functions as well.

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:35.020
One function is updating
the purchase log,

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:37.510
which is in some data solution.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.690
In this case, Bigtable.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:43.110
Another function in the
same sequence updating

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:46.830
state in a different location,
like in the user purchase

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:47.850
database.

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:49.980
So see here, we're
making use of things

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:52.990
like functions to do
things very easily.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:56.760
And when you're building
your new microservices,

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.650
taking advantage of
these sort of patterns

00:28:58.650 --> 00:29:00.720
will help you a lot
in terms of being

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:06.484
able to add new capabilities
or new features very quickly.

00:29:06.484 --> 00:29:08.900
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: OK.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:13.420
So, that's a nice,
big red slide.

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:18.200
When things go wrong,
they will always--

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.530
so one thing that's
always mentioned

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:27.320
in these kind of things
is the culture change

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:29.760
that happens if you're
running a monolith dev team

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:32.600
to a microservice dev team.

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:35.450
And as we migrate more of those
services over to the left-hand

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:38.300
side, team's going to--

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.280
that number is
going to grow, yeah?

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:42.950
This case, we may have like six.

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:45.570
But what if that was 600?

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:48.780
The teams basically-- they
take ownership of that tech

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:50.940
stack [INAUDIBLE]
whether that service is.

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.640
So right up to pager duty.

00:29:52.640 --> 00:29:55.380
Now if you have 600
microservices, how on earth

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:57.380
is the dev team looking
after user auth going

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:00.120
to have any information
about how the whole wider

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.635
system works?

00:30:02.635 --> 00:30:04.510
One of the things that
you don't want to have

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:06.730
is dev teams that
are working in silos.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:10.060
Nobody likes that, and
that's never productive.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:14.800
So what we know is we're moving
to microservice and complexity

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.950
increases.

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:21.560
And as they own the whole of
the service, what we don't

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:24.020
want to have is accidents.

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:26.590
We will have accidents, but
we want to try and avoid them.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:31.410
So one particular example of
this is the recursion problem.

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:34.880
Hey, service team, the team
that looks after service A,

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:36.380
we're going to
deploy a new feature.

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:37.879
Great, we're going
to use service B,

00:30:37.879 --> 00:30:39.250
because that's really cool.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:41.750
We didn't know that service B
called service C that actually

00:30:41.750 --> 00:30:44.990
calls service A. And now,
Hussain's latency problem

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:48.720
is a real big problem.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:50.997
So how do we fix this?

00:30:50.997 --> 00:30:52.580
Now, one thing that's
really important

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:55.310
is that developers
document their service.

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:57.320
And I'm not talking about
inline documentation,

00:30:57.320 --> 00:30:59.740
which all of its developers
did that other time.

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:01.220
But they're not
very good at this.

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.970
I'm talking about having some
sort of open and accessible

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:07.280
place for every
single service as you

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:10.010
migrate, where
everybody knows where

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:11.930
to find information about it--

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:14.810
What it does, so what
you expect it to do,

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:18.980
what dependencies it has, and
include which other service it

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:21.830
calls and make it discoverable.

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:25.476
This is not technical,
but this is important.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:31.680
It's adding a lot of complexity.

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:32.720
We keep using that word.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:34.610
Complexity,
complexity, complexity.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:38.060
And we're thinking, OK, we've
got the business justification

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:40.670
to move to
microservices, but it's

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:45.070
sounding like a
pretty hard, long slog

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:46.550
to move to microservices.

00:31:46.550 --> 00:31:47.330
So why do it?

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:51.240
Well, I'm going to repurpose
a real situation that happened

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:53.774
to me quite a few years ago.

00:31:53.774 --> 00:31:59.700
I'm going to restyle it
in the e-commerce example.

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:01.490
But it was running
on App Engine.

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:03.404
It was a small monolith, right?

00:32:03.404 --> 00:32:04.320
We're using Datastore.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:07.010
State was in Memcache,
you know, and data store.

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:08.260
It was a distributed monolith.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:11.650
It wasn't terrible.

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:12.810
Code base, quite small.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.007
Nothing-- no surprises there.

00:32:17.007 --> 00:32:18.090
You can see how old it is.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:19.673
If any front-end
developers are there,

00:32:19.673 --> 00:32:22.314
can you spot something sort
of which takes us back?

00:32:22.314 --> 00:32:23.730
There's a nice
grunt file in there

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:25.320
that really takes us back.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:27.740
If you're not a front-end
dev, you probably don't care.

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:31.080
Anyway, so all of this
had simple scripts

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:36.150
that run on the deployment
pipeline, a few minor tasks

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:38.670
bundled up all to JavaScript,
deployed out to App Engine.

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:43.440
And in this case, we've done
a major deploy to days ago.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:46.020
It wasn't on a Friday,
and it wasn't 5:00 PM.

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:47.837
So we did have
actually the ability

00:32:47.837 --> 00:32:49.420
to know that it
wasn't going to crash,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:51.390
and it seemed to be
performing really well.

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:52.130
So, great.

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:54.250
We can start working
on the next features.

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:55.501
And I go home in the evening.

00:32:55.501 --> 00:32:57.000
You know where this
is going, right?

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:57.650
Yeah?

00:32:57.650 --> 00:32:59.930
I go home in the
evening, go to bed.

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:03.600
2:00 AM, mobile goes off,
a really unhappy person

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.420
on the other end of the phone.

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:06.270
Website's down.

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:08.420
And I'm thinking, hang on.

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:11.487
Who's deploying it
2:00 AM in the morning?

00:33:11.487 --> 00:33:13.820
Luckily, being a monolithic,
I've got one place to look.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:15.945
And being in App Engine,
I go straight to the logs,

00:33:15.945 --> 00:33:18.590
and I can see that there is
one file to break them all.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:20.850
And it was config.py

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:23.840
I know that doesn't
sound that great,

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:26.600
but what was in there
was some hacky code.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:28.310
Nobody ever has that, right?

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:31.850
We had some hacky
code that worked,

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:34.651
was in the backlog to be
fixed, and to make less hacky.

00:33:34.651 --> 00:33:36.650
But because it worked,
it never got prioritized.

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:39.680
And what that code
did is it told

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:42.287
the front-end what
state it should be in,

00:33:42.287 --> 00:33:43.370
what mode it should be in.

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:47.900
So let's say for e-commerce,
it was on this date.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.750
From this date, we should
be in Black Friday mode.

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:51.980
On this date, Cyber Monday.

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:52.549
You get it.

00:33:52.549 --> 00:33:53.840
On this date, blah, blah, blah.

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:56.490
The problem was is in
that deploy two days ago,

00:33:56.490 --> 00:33:59.430
somehow it got
unnoticed, but some dev

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.605
had changed the date
to the last date

00:34:02.605 --> 00:34:04.220
that midnight was just past.

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:07.130
And because there was no
exception around the error,

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:10.264
the whole application went, ah.

00:34:10.264 --> 00:34:12.389
I don't know what state
the front-end should be in,

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:14.659
so I'm going to show
a nice 500 error.

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:16.595
And it's the App
Engine 500 error.

00:34:16.595 --> 00:34:18.469
If you haven't got a
default template set up,

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:20.690
you know how ugly that is.

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:23.810
Luckily, it's easy fix.

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:25.434
Just fix the date, deploy it.

00:34:25.434 --> 00:34:26.600
Up and running, no problems.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:27.659
Go back to bed.

00:34:27.659 --> 00:34:31.050
Well, afterwards we did
a quick post-mortem.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:33.620
There was two key
things that we decided

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:35.059
that we learned out of this.

00:34:35.059 --> 00:34:36.949
Our alerting needed
to be much better.

00:34:36.949 --> 00:34:39.062
Why did our alerting
need to be much better?

00:34:39.062 --> 00:34:41.270
Because somebody had to
phone me, which wasn't great,

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:44.120
because it was my boss.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:48.489
But the fact is why did
it take two hours for me

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:50.100
not to notice that?

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:52.690
Well, we had front-end cache
headers on all of the logged

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:53.380
out pages.

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:57.040
So all of the non-user sessions,
yeah, it looked like it worked.

00:34:57.040 --> 00:34:58.720
And at 2:00 AM,
there isn't very many

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:00.700
logged in users buying stuff.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.200
So our alerting
need to much better,

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:05.230
because we need to know
as soon as that happens

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:08.570
that we need to handle it.

00:35:08.570 --> 00:35:11.640
And it was a really
terrible user experience.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:14.790
The first one is actually
a lot easier in a way

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:17.640
when you have a single
service App Engine.

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.906
If you had 600
microservices running,

00:35:20.906 --> 00:35:23.280
you need to really make sure
that your alerting is better

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:25.950
and you can understand
the problems.

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:27.200
OK, so what do we do next?

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:30.790
What we did, we got all display
logic, stuck in an Angular

00:35:30.790 --> 00:35:34.410
wrap, and gave it its own
service, complete 100% static,

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:35.880
no Python anymore.

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:39.510
The backend, we quickly
made APIs for everything.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:41.400
We had-- well, it
says less monolithic.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:44.440
We're still a monolith, it just
wasn't working quite the same.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:46.050
No display logic in it.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.360
We still have the same
problem, though, right?

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:49.860
One file, one exception.

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:50.970
Whole thing goes down.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:55.170
But we have a real
good improvement now.

00:35:55.170 --> 00:35:56.910
The front-end
client can go, hey,

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.190
the backend has decided to die.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.470
I'm going to show a picture
of a puppy or whatever.

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:07.960
I can do something better than
the default App Engine page.

00:36:07.960 --> 00:36:10.700
So what do we do next?

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:12.450
Well, we actually
started breaking the app

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:14.820
into domain-specific APIs.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:22.680
And this gives us the ability
to more intelligently handle

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:25.710
errors and keep the users
unaware that everything's

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:27.130
on fire.

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:30.600
OK, so we got six things there.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:32.820
And now, I'm going back
to the 600 things, right?

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:35.640
That's going to be
really hard to manage

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:37.560
the combination of
errors that possibly

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:41.730
could happen in a
600-microservice environment.

00:36:41.730 --> 00:36:46.160
So you need you need to
identify your critical paths

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:49.436
in your microservice
environment.

00:36:49.436 --> 00:36:51.060
So what does a critical
path look like?

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:52.694
And us, we want
people to buy things,

00:36:52.694 --> 00:36:54.110
because otherwise
there's no money

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:57.410
to pay for the web development,
and we're all unemployed.

00:36:57.410 --> 00:36:59.630
So user comes to
the checkout page,

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:01.670
and the user cart
service, the thing

00:37:01.670 --> 00:37:05.870
that tells what the user has
put in their cart is down, OK?

00:37:05.870 --> 00:37:08.670
Well, what we can do is we could
have multiple projects for App

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:10.820
Engine, and we could
deploy those services out

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:12.140
to multiple regions.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:13.860
And the client can go--

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:15.470
I'm in US East.

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:17.550
I'm going to try US West.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:18.890
OK, that's OK.

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:21.950
What happens if that disappears?

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:23.850
OK, so what do we do now?

00:37:23.850 --> 00:37:26.700
Well, we need to make sure
that people buy things.

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:34.200
So shopping carts, they used
to use cookies to track.

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.060
In a multi-device era,
that doesn't work,

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:37.476
so that's why we
have the service.

00:37:37.476 --> 00:37:38.570
But let's not discount it.

00:37:38.570 --> 00:37:40.445
We could use local
storage, and we could keep

00:37:40.445 --> 00:37:42.270
the client keeping a record.

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:44.500
Maybe we know when the
two services are out,

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:46.327
they're actually--

00:37:46.327 --> 00:37:48.410
we'll say a message to say
it may no be the latest

00:37:48.410 --> 00:37:49.785
information, but
the key thing is

00:37:49.785 --> 00:37:53.215
the user can carry on
and make that purchase.

00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:59.210
So you can see there that
wasn't the microservice team

00:37:59.210 --> 00:38:02.010
necessarily that
actually had to fix that.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:05.600
It was the people who were
looking after the front-end UI.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:09.020
So you need to make sure that
the critical paths are jointly

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:12.320
owned and actually
properly owned by the teams

00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:13.930
that it effects.

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:18.260
You know, don't be blinkered
down one technical path either.

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:20.510
There may be multiple
solutions, and the thing

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:23.570
that should drive
those solutions

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:25.200
should be the business.

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:26.900
What is it you are trying to do?

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.500
I can' see we're running
a bit out of time,

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.310
so I'm going to speed up.

00:38:31.310 --> 00:38:33.600
OK, so the user
services was down.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:34.840
Right, that's really simple.

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:36.960
But actually, why was
the use of service down?

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:38.170
The user had caught service.

00:38:38.170 --> 00:38:39.860
It was because the
user service was out.

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:41.510
And let's say we
didn't go to Firebase,

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:43.580
and we still have that
relational database.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:45.290
That relational database is out.

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:47.820
OK, so now we have even more
teams on the critical path.

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:49.350
We have the people looking
after the database,

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:50.540
we have the user service.

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:51.390
What can we do?

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:57.920
Well, you know, we could have
an explosion of services dying,

00:38:57.920 --> 00:39:00.890
because, as Hussain said,
the user service is atomic.

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:03.290
So now, we have
lots of complexity

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:04.550
on what has just happened.

00:39:04.550 --> 00:39:07.550
One service is down and our
whole platform is going out.

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:12.820
And It's feeling like
microservices is hard work.

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:15.340
OK, so we'll file over
to a read replica.

00:39:15.340 --> 00:39:16.010
We'll limp on.

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:17.525
We'll be in read-only mode.

00:39:17.525 --> 00:39:20.150
We won't do logins, and all the
clients will stop doing logins.

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:23.640
But users that are currently
in the system, that's fine.

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:26.570
OK, so region one, we
send to region two.

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:29.150
Region two is now
sending both things.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:32.630
And yeah, welcome to your
self-created denial of service

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:33.890
attack on region two.

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:35.530
And everything is out.

00:39:35.530 --> 00:39:39.910
Hey, I thought this
was a good idea, right?

00:39:39.910 --> 00:39:43.562
OK so let's just take a
step back a little bit.

00:39:43.562 --> 00:39:44.770
Things you ought to remember.

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:46.950
Again, six services, not 600.

00:39:46.950 --> 00:39:48.692
Let's think how complex
this is with 600.

00:39:48.692 --> 00:39:50.150
I'm not going to
do that, actually.

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:51.946
Let's keep it simple with six.

00:39:51.946 --> 00:39:53.570
You can see that
actually request three

00:39:53.570 --> 00:39:56.350
to-- even a service that
isn't directly relying on it

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:57.260
is going to be out.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:00.470
Everybody has to understand
what the error situation is.

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:04.340
And what you need to do
is you need to file fast.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:07.531
What if you had timeouts set
at five minutes for all these?

00:40:07.531 --> 00:40:08.780
All the requests will balloon.

00:40:08.780 --> 00:40:09.890
Oh, that's OK.

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:11.180
We're using serverless, right?

00:40:11.180 --> 00:40:12.700
We can scale.

00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:14.910
Oh, you're paying
for that, you know?

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:16.850
So you need to fail fast.

00:40:16.850 --> 00:40:19.160
You need to send an error
message so that everything

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:19.829
in your system--

00:40:19.829 --> 00:40:21.620
and it should be part
of your documentation

00:40:21.620 --> 00:40:24.974
too-- understands what it
should do in this situation.

00:40:24.974 --> 00:40:26.390
In this situation,
actually, we're

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:28.110
going to throw in an
extra piece of technology.

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:30.568
And this is probably a good
time to throw in an extra piece

00:40:30.568 --> 00:40:31.180
of technology.

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:34.817
We're going to use an
in-memory database.

00:40:34.817 --> 00:40:35.900
We know we can scale that.

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:37.320
We could use Cloud Memorystore.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:41.550
I mean, we know we're not
going to DoS another region.

00:40:41.550 --> 00:40:44.690
So to do this, maybe we used
the event sourcing idea.

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:47.684
We have two cloud functions
pushing to both databases.

00:40:47.684 --> 00:40:49.100
What we can do,
and the reason why

00:40:49.100 --> 00:40:52.460
the amber is the
user service knows

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:54.170
that we're in file over mode.

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:56.086
And it's going to send
an error message, which

00:40:56.086 --> 00:41:00.156
tells everything, that we're
only in a read-only situation.

00:41:00.156 --> 00:41:01.780
Hopefully, that's
given you enough time

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:03.340
to restore that database.

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:05.930
Now, if this was a user
service and it was me,

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:08.080
I would probably make
the in-memory database

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:12.310
the primary database and have
another database as a fallback,

00:41:12.310 --> 00:41:15.700
because, as Hussain pointed
out earlier, it was atomic.

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:17.650
It's going to be
very, very chatty.

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:20.470
So the advantages of having
that really low latency

00:41:20.470 --> 00:41:23.150
is going to be important.

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:27.090
OK, speeding through.

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:30.040
So microservice and faults.

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:31.712
It's complicated, right?

00:41:31.712 --> 00:41:36.720
But with that complexity,
we have more options, right?

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:40.320
We can safely say that even
though that is more complex,

00:41:40.320 --> 00:41:43.620
we can give the user a much
better user experience.

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:46.380
If the user has a much
better user experience,

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:52.160
they're more likely to stay with
your website or your platform.

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:53.264
Circuit breakers.

00:41:53.264 --> 00:41:54.680
If you all know
microservices, I'm

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:56.010
going to explain what
one of those are.

00:41:56.010 --> 00:41:57.593
But it's important
that you have them.

00:41:57.593 --> 00:42:00.200
And maybe you limit
the amount of languages

00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:02.760
you have in your platform, so
you have standardized circuit

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:03.260
breakers.

00:42:03.260 --> 00:42:09.030
You know, fail fast
and have a plan B.

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:11.060
Bulkheads.

00:42:11.060 --> 00:42:13.820
Create partitions
between the domains.

00:42:13.820 --> 00:42:17.780
Make sure that when you have
that catastrophic failure,

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:19.760
your whole system
doesn't go down.

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:24.800
Now, the bulkheads thing comes
from boats or a submarine,

00:42:24.800 --> 00:42:29.540
where you put a bulkhead
between the different partitions

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:32.540
to stop the whole boat sinking.

00:42:32.540 --> 00:42:35.150
But I want you to take away--

00:42:35.150 --> 00:42:39.710
if you're going into your
board, you're going to your CTO,

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:43.350
or you're advising a
customer to go into--

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:44.880
you're going to go
on this journey.

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:46.879
You don't want to show
them these slides, right?

00:42:46.879 --> 00:42:48.560
Because that may
frighten them, right?

00:42:48.560 --> 00:42:52.880
So how can you explain this
migration process and handling

00:42:52.880 --> 00:42:55.570
all of this to a
non-technical person?

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:59.142
OK, so is it anybody's
birthday today?

00:42:59.142 --> 00:43:01.106
No?

00:43:01.106 --> 00:43:06.020
[AIR BLOWING]

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:09.420
Sure it's nobody's birthday?

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:14.030
Let's say the business objective
is to hold a volume of air.

00:43:14.030 --> 00:43:18.510
This is your volume of
air, thank you very much.

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:22.540
This is your monolith, and the
volume of air is your logic.

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:23.360
[BALLOON POPS]

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:24.720
There we go, one error.

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:25.350
It's gone down.

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:25.710
Sorry.

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:26.834
Did that land on your head?

00:43:26.834 --> 00:43:28.620
[HUSSAIN LAUGHS]

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:30.970
This is bubble wrap, right?

00:43:30.970 --> 00:43:34.750
We can hold the same volume
of air with our bubble wrap.

00:43:34.750 --> 00:43:38.396
But wasn't it a lot easier
to understand the balloon?

00:43:38.396 --> 00:43:40.240
It had a nice shape.

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:41.840
We could understand it.

00:43:41.840 --> 00:43:45.560
Microservices, I don't
know what this is.

00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:48.160
It's multi-layered, but I'm
holding the same volume of air,

00:43:48.160 --> 00:43:48.970
roughly.

00:43:48.970 --> 00:43:52.090
Caveat, big needle.

00:43:52.090 --> 00:43:55.270
And probably if I get
addicted to popping the cells.

00:43:55.270 --> 00:43:56.530
You get the point, right?

00:43:56.530 --> 00:43:59.080
We're only going through
those certain things,

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:02.330
and the rest of the
system stays functioning.

00:44:02.330 --> 00:44:03.910
That has got to
be your objective.

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:05.830
And as you migrate
from your monolith

00:44:05.830 --> 00:44:08.884
with all of the culture
change and with everything,

00:44:08.884 --> 00:44:11.050
try and keep that in your
head, because that's where

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:13.730
you want to try and achieve to.

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:15.936
And with that,
I'll pass you back.

00:44:15.936 --> 00:44:17.110
Hang on.

00:44:17.110 --> 00:44:18.151
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: Yeah?

00:44:18.151 --> 00:44:19.117
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:19.117 --> 00:44:20.200
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Oh, yeah.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:21.200
I've got one more thing.

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:23.070
Hold on, Hussain.

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:24.460
Semantic versioning.

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:25.450
Got five minutes.

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:26.350
We're good.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:28.630
Semantic versioning.

00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:30.400
Everybody knows
what this is, yes?

00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:32.390
Anybody doesn't know
this, put your hands up.

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:34.000
Don't be shy.

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:35.440
OK, go look it up
on the internet.

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:35.920
It's right there.

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:36.545
Take a picture.

00:44:36.545 --> 00:44:38.845
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:43.120
Why is it important and
why am I doing that?

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:46.870
Well, because this is
your contract to everybody

00:44:46.870 --> 00:44:48.052
who uses your service.

00:44:48.052 --> 00:44:49.760
You never had to do
this with a monolith.

00:44:49.760 --> 00:44:51.926
Well, actually, you might
have had a version number,

00:44:51.926 --> 00:44:55.240
but it could have been
kind of meaningless.

00:44:55.240 --> 00:45:00.520
Here, if that service team
A wanted to use service B,

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:05.350
they could see which version
they had started using.

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:07.750
You probably want to
have a deprecation policy

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:10.090
throughout the
organization, so you

00:45:10.090 --> 00:45:13.130
understand that if somebody
changes the major version

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:15.010
and they have a
breaking change, you

00:45:15.010 --> 00:45:16.990
know you have a year, right?

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.420
Or six months or
whatever you choose

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:21.700
to put that on the
backlog and get that

00:45:21.700 --> 00:45:23.530
changed in all of the services.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:26.450
Without this, you have
the classic problem

00:45:26.450 --> 00:45:29.710
of somebody making a
backward, incompatible change,

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:31.460
deploying that into production.

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:34.780
You're back to that situation,
where everything dies.

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:38.082
So make sure you use
semantic versioning.

00:45:40.980 --> 00:45:46.620
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: So
let's review where we are.

00:45:46.620 --> 00:45:49.650
What have we achieved with our
monolithic application that we

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:52.800
started with, the
one that only had

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:59.040
a load balancer and that one
instance and a data store?

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:01.640
We added a new web front UI.

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:04.970
We added a bunch of
new APIs and migrated

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:08.060
something difficult like
auth into a microservice.

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:11.540
We identified a bunch of easy
wins with the edge capabilities

00:46:11.540 --> 00:46:13.400
and identified
the difficult ones

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:15.570
with the sticky capabilities.

00:46:15.570 --> 00:46:19.040
We also looked at error
handling and identified

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:21.380
what critical paths are.

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:23.540
And right now, we have
a deeper understanding

00:46:23.540 --> 00:46:25.005
of what the system is.

00:46:25.005 --> 00:46:26.130
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: Hopefully.

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:28.160
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI:
Yeah, hopefully.

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:30.300
So what do we do next?

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:31.700
What's after this?

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:33.480
You need to continue
your journey.

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:36.950
We don't really have a
full-fledged microservice

00:46:36.950 --> 00:46:38.570
system, nor we have a monolith.

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:40.870
We have something in between.

00:46:40.870 --> 00:46:44.330
The idea is take
what we've done here

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:47.580
and iterate, follow
the same process.

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:50.440
Extract services,
extract capabilities out,

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:51.680
turn them to microservices.

00:46:51.680 --> 00:46:55.443
And it's a continuous
way to evolve.

00:46:55.443 --> 00:46:57.910
You kill different
parts of the monolith

00:46:57.910 --> 00:47:01.000
and create new
microservices and do it

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:04.460
at your own pace to avoid
any of the issues we saw.

00:47:04.460 --> 00:47:06.590
And one thing that's
important to think about

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:09.830
is the culture of change
that this will have on your--

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:11.720
culture change that this will--

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.670
how this will impact
your organization.

00:47:14.670 --> 00:47:17.040
Your organization could
have been a monolith, where

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:19.350
you have one big
team that manages

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:21.000
this monolithic application.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:22.440
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: And we could
have done 15 minutes just

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:22.950
on that, right?

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:23.580
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: Yeah.

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:24.454
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: So--

00:47:24.454 --> 00:47:27.697
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: And right
now, what's going to happen

00:47:27.697 --> 00:47:29.280
is that you're going
to have services,

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:30.660
different microservices.

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:34.020
And you're going to have
different teams managing

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:37.060
those microservices as well.

00:47:37.060 --> 00:47:39.690
So make sure to embrace
that, because that's

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:42.160
the best way to move forward.

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:44.970
So good luck on your journey.

00:47:44.970 --> 00:47:47.280
Understand why you're
making the decisions

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:51.900
you are and be aware that things
will go wrong, so be careful

00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:53.480
while you're moving along.

00:47:53.480 --> 00:47:54.720
Go on your own pace.

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:56.410
Don't try to do
this all at once.

00:47:56.410 --> 00:47:58.650
This is an iterative process.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:48:01.860
And make sure you balance
between business motivations

00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:07.347
and whatever trade-offs
and risks you're taking.

00:48:07.347 --> 00:48:09.180
Thank you very much for
attending this talk.

00:48:09.180 --> 00:48:10.390
JUSTIN GRAYSTON: We
have two minutes.

00:48:10.390 --> 00:48:11.810
Does anybody want to do a QA?

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:12.851
HUSSAIN AL MUSCATI: Yeah.

00:48:12.851 --> 00:48:13.450
